WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.220
Still upset over the prospect of never getting
to see Zack Snyder's version of Justice League?

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.910
Well, grab your tissues, because a newly released
image from Snyder's version of the superhero

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.660
film has teased a heartbreaking death that'll
be sure to get you welling up.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.060
Taking to the social media platform Vero,
Snyder shared a still from a scene of his

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.900
cut of Justice League, which reveals a crucial
plot point in Cyborg's storyline.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:26.020
The black-and-white shot shows Cyborg with
his hand pressed against a pane of glass,

00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:27.679
looking on as his father, S.T.A.R.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.120
Labs head Dr. Silas Stone, is apparently burnt
alive in what appears to be a containment

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:33.620
chamber.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.340
It seems the fire may have been a result of
an explosion that involved a Mother Box, a

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.800
powerful piece of technology that appeared
in both Snyder's Justice League and the theatrical

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.440
version directed by Joss Whedon.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.920
Snyder had planned to explore an alternate
history of the Mother Boxes, and has even

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.260
shared storyboard art that suggested his Justice
League would have seen Cyborg discussing the

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.500
Boxes' inception, and their connection to
the Nazis during World War II.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.280
Snyder captioned the still from Silas' death
scene, "You would also cry" teasing the effect

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.700
his take on Justice League would have on audiences.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.180
And watching a beloved hero witness the death
of his father from just a few feet away would

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.520
no doubt be devastating, so Snyder isn't wrong
there.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.480
It's obvious that Snyder's vision for Justice
League was going to be far darker than Whedon's,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.210
and including a death as emotional as the
one intended for Silas would have brought

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.040
the film to a somber place indeed.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.999
But evidently that isn't what studio Warner
Bros. wanted out of Justice League particularly

00:01:27.999 --> 00:01:32.869
because its predecessor, Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice, was slammed as a trainwreck

00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:34.940
largely thanks to its gritty tone.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.720
Rumors about Silas dying in Justice League
swirled around the production for months before

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.819
its November 2017 debut and have continued
to the present day, with many speculating

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.000
that his death would establish the foundation
for an eventual Cyborg solo film.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:54.439
A pair of August 2018 tweets from The New
York Times' Kyle Buchanan, who cited a report

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.440
by Slashfilm, claimed that Silas did indeed
die in the original version of Justice League.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:00.580
Buchanan wrote:

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.799
"Originally, Joe Morton's character was killed
halfway through, and Cyborg finds a video

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.390
from him at the end that lets Morton's lines
play over the final montage.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.800
Whedon gave those lines to Amy Adams.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.460
But Whedon's reshoots also nixed the Morton
death scene, so...a wash?"

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.060
In that Slashfilm report, Silas actor Joe
Morton confirmed that he filmed a ton of scenes

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.200
for Snyder's Justice League that were scrapped
when Whedon took over.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.560
Morton maintained his hope that the sequences
removed from Justice League would be reworked

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.700
for the Cyborg standalone, and also told IGN
that part of the Justice League reshoots involved

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.600
adjusting Cyborg's tone to, quote, "lighten
up the film."

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.620
He explained:

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.080
"There was [more we shot].

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.700
They completely changed the story.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.180
Unfortunately, I can't tell you what [the
original story] is because that might end

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.070
up being in part of the Cyborg movie.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.460
Yes, there was a big change once they decided
that they were going to do the Cyborg film."

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:02.340
But whether the Cyborg movie ever ends up
happening, and whether the film will actually

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.340
include the Silas death scene Snyder teased,
is a mystery for right now.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.900
According to Cyborg star Ray Fisher, the standalone
superhero flick, quote, "would be a very,

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:16.880
very costly movie to make because it is so
CGI heavy" which could explain why there hasn't

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:18.100
been much movement on it.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.680
Unfortunately, rumors recently began to surface
suggesting that the Cyborg film is actually

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.660
dead in the water.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.440
We Got This Covered cited unidentified sources
claiming Warner Bros. "just doesn't care about

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.190
making any more projects with Fisher."

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.160
Ouch.

